00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:06.019
Now, one of the major things that data science
tries to do is

00:04:06.019 --> 00:04:10.180
automate the processes that we do every day.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:14.269
And so one of the things that I'd like to
do is try to help us predict

00:04:14.269 --> 00:04:16.889
breast cancer.

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:23.050
So I've got my data science console up here,
and you guys should be able to see that.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.889
And what
I'm going to do is head over to the community,

00:04:25.889 --> 00:04:27.740
and I'm going to look up the data set.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:32.889
And that data set is a breast
cancer data set that comes from the University

00:04:32.889 --> 00:04:34.590
of California, Irvine.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:36.430
Let's just put it in here.

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:39.729
And I'll let you take a
quick look at it.

00:04:39.729 --> 00:04:45.110
What you'll see is that in this data set,
you've got a patient identifier.

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:53.360
You've got a diagnosis of either benign or
malignant tumors.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:57.020
And then we've taken structured data that
we've gathered from the X-ray.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:01.740
Now, some of you
might be thinking, well, data science on highly

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.530
structured data is easy.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:07.680
Teach a computer how to recognize it from
a picture of an X-ray-- and that's what I'll

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.759
be talking about in the second portion of
this-- deep learning and

00:05:10.759 --> 00:05:14.039
convolutional networks, those types of things.

00:05:14.039 --> 00:05:17.490
But to set a baseline, what I really wanted
to do is see if I could teach a computer to

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:24.190
recognize breast cancer the
same way a doctor would by looking at an X-ray.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:27.210
So you can see, we've got about 32 columns.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.170
We've got a few
hundred examples.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:34.909
And these columns, other than the diagnosis,
are details that we've taken from the X-ray.

00:05:34.909 --> 00:05:37.960
For
example, if we see a malignant tumor, what's

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:39.449
the texture of that tumor?

00:05:39.449 --> 00:05:41.210
What's the perimeter around it?

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:43.490
How
much area does it take up?

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:46.910
So highly structured data so far.

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:50.669
So what I'm going to do is I'm going to create
a project for this.

00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:52.699
I've called it NIH video.

00:05:52.699 --> 00:05:56.300
Now, we are talking about
scalable data science.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:02.919
So everything I'm doing here, you'll notice,
is written in either Python or R. And I create

00:06:02.919 --> 00:06:03.919
notebooks.

00:06:03.919 --> 00:06:08.810
Now, if you're not familiar with notebooks,
IBM thinks that they are going to be absolutely

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:15.240
the sort of
standard for how we share analytics and code.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.090
And what's interesting about a notebook, as
you can see here, a

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:21.199
notebook looks a lot like a Word document.

00:06:21.199 --> 00:06:26.629
This particular notebook is a Jupyter Notebook,
which, again, supports Python, R, Sparks,

00:06:26.629 --> 00:06:28.800
Scala, those types of
languages.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.639
And what's interesting about them is that
you actually can write code and execute code

00:06:33.639 --> 00:06:34.900
and pull data
from these.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:38.930
So we'll be working in a notebook environment
today.

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:41.610
I won't make it pretty for the sake of us
actually

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:46.419
getting to the data science part of it, but
if you are interested in taking a look at

00:06:46.419 --> 00:06:50.699
what a professionally-authored
notebook with professional data is, you could

00:06:50.699 --> 00:06:55.189
go take a look for, again, classifying tumors
with machine learning.

00:06:55.189 --> 00:06:59.360
So one thing I want to do is exploratory data
analysis.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:01.949
So what I've done is I've downloaded that
data set that I

00:07:01.949 --> 00:07:05.819
pointed out earlier-- Breast Cancer Wisconsin
Data Set.

00:07:05.819 --> 00:07:10.620
So you can do your exploratory data analysis
wherever

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:14.300
you want, but I'm going to just do it within
the Data Science Experience here.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:19.120
I can also show you how you might
do it with things like PixieDust in Python.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:22.419
But I want to generate some basic metrics
about things.

00:07:22.419 --> 00:07:32.629
So first of all, we can see here that out
of our cases, 357 were benign, and 212 were

00:07:32.629 --> 00:07:35.530
malignant, which means that
we've got a slight imbalance of data there.

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:37.889
That's probably something we'll need to take
into account.

00:07:37.889 --> 00:07:41.020
And as we
look across the others, we don't see any kind

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:42.569
of a normal distribution.

00:07:42.569 --> 00:07:46.410
We just see data, which is kind of data.

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:50.720
So if
we're trying to predict benign or malignant,

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:53.090
you've got a couple of different options.

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:57.060
You could go with-- an
example would be a logistic regression, which

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:00.379
is really good at deciding between two binary
things.

00:08:00.379 --> 00:08:06.439
But because of our data and how it can be
sort of non-linear, non-parametric, we're

00:08:06.439 --> 00:08:08.419
going to go with a tree-based
approach.

00:08:08.419 --> 00:08:10.689
And I'll get into what that means later.

00:08:10.689 --> 00:08:13.650
If we needed to visualize something, we could
do that here, but

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:18.099
what I'm going to do is create a notebook.

00:08:18.099 --> 00:08:21.650
And I'm going to show you the steps you go
through to produce that

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:25.949
model, and then we'll move again to the deep
learning stuff.

00:08:25.949 --> 00:08:29.340
So I'm going to create a new notebook.

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:31.349
And I'm doing
this deliberately a little bit slowly so that

00:08:31.349 --> 00:08:34.380
if you are following along, you can do so.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:39.889
I'm going to call this Predicting Breast Cancer.

00:08:39.889 --> 00:08:41.520
I want to write in Python.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:47.610
R works just as well, but what I want to
point out here is that everything we're doing

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:49.310
is running on Spark.

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:57.639
So underneath the covers, we're writing Python,
but it's executing as Pi Spark or SparkR.

00:08:57.639 --> 00:09:00.890
And if you're not familiar with Spark, it's
incredibly important to the big

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:02.510
data space.

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:07.760
What Spark is is an engine which is in memory,
which is distributed, which is designed to

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:09.200
do a variety
of different topics.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:12.400
So you could have Spark do machine learning.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:15.790
You can do graph analytics with Spark.

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:18.130
You can do SQL-like
analytics.

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:23.070
But today, were primarily going to be using
Python to do machine learning [? models.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:26.630
?] So the first
thing I'm going to do is pull in that data

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:28.620
set that I just got.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.790
So I can do a header here.

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:44.900
We can say, this is my
notebook, and we're going to predict cancer.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:49.940
Then add-- whoa-- add a new cell.

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:52.220
And we can get off to the races.

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:55.970
So what's interesting about these is each
of these can be individually executed.

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:58.360
So I need to pull in my data.

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:00.000
I'm
going to go over here to the data tab.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.690
And everything that you see in blue here,
by the way, is sort of unique to the

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:04.740
Data Science Experience.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:09.449
So there's some nice things up here around
data scheduling and collaboration, but

00:10:09.449 --> 00:10:14.310
what I want to do is insert my data set that
I have in this project as a [INAUDIBLE] data

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:15.360
frame.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.440
Now, by the way,
anybody that wants to get access to this project,

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:23.560
just send me an email, and I can add you as
a collaborator.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:25.839
You'll
be able to see all the code, all the data,

00:10:25.839 --> 00:10:27.850
and work with me on it as we [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:35.530
Now, this code actually generates [INAUDIBLE]
code, which is really slick, but at the same

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:38.310
time, it shows my
password to me.

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:39.310
Not to everybody else.

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:42.510
But since we're recording this, I'm going
to take a moment to pause the

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:45.900
screen, generate my code, and then I'll delete
it.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:11:04.860
So I'm pausing the screen now.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:11.230
Give me one moment please.

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:24.470
I'm
just making some adjustments to the code so

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:26.720
that we get a good data set.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:32.730
And then, again, we'll be good to go.

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:43.860
OK, hold on.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:50.870
By the way, if you're interested in, again,
following along, you can use-- let's see--

00:11:50.870 --> 00:11:59.779
this beautiful notebook that I'm
about to put on the screen over here while

00:11:59.779 --> 00:12:08.399
I'm generating my code.

00:12:08.399 --> 00:12:16.190
Hm.

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:24.339
So I tell you what.

00:12:24.339 --> 00:12:28.079
Let's actually walk
through doing this the old-fashioned way,

00:12:28.079 --> 00:12:30.029
just so you can see it.

00:12:30.029 --> 00:12:34.430
So what I'm going to do is take my breast
cancer

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:52.839
data set that you saw me download earlier--
I put it in here in my NIH video demo folder--

00:12:52.839 --> 00:13:01.220
and I'm going to drag
and drop right onto my screen here.

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:02.750
You'll see I've uploaded it.

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:05.550
You'll see it appears down here.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:06.949
There we go.

00:13:06.949 --> 00:13:12.410
And I'm going to create that notebook
again.

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:25.040
So we're going to call this Predicting Cancer,
create the notebook, and then do the code

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:33.800
generation.

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:35.570
There
we go.

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:37.139
I'm going to run it.

00:13:37.139 --> 00:13:38.670
And you can see, I have my data.

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:46.930
So, again, I've got my identifier, my diagnosis,
and then other data sets.

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:49.779
So how do we move forward?

00:13:49.779 --> 00:13:52.360
Well, the
first thing I'm going to do is rename this

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:57.490
data file just to be something simple, because
I don't like typing that every

00:13:57.490 --> 00:13:59.050
single time.

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:00.959
So we're just going to re-execute this.

00:14:00.959 --> 00:14:02.190
All right.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:06.250
Now we move into the phase of what's called
exploratory data analysis.

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:12.350
Now, I like to use a tool called PixieDust
to do this, which is a Python tool.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:14.589
And to make
sure that we've got the proper libraries,

00:14:14.589 --> 00:14:16.550
I'm going to install PixieDust here.

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:18.819
Here we go.

00:14:18.819 --> 00:14:22.870
It looks like I'm good to go
on that front.

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:28.010
I'm going to tell my notebook that I want
to use PixieDust now that I've got it installed.

00:14:28.010 --> 00:14:32.760
So I import,
and now I can use the display call.

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:37.280
So this is a function which actually generates
something similar to what you

00:14:37.280 --> 00:14:43.220
might get with Excel or a tool that does charts
and graphs.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:49.450
So I can say, I want to do a bar chart, and
I want to do it based on-- I don't know.

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:54.380
We could do the ID, and we can
do diagnosis here.

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:57.449
And so I can do some exploratory data analysis
in this way.

00:14:57.449 --> 00:14:59.569
I can look at distributions, things
like that.

00:14:59.569 --> 00:15:01.050
Now, we talked about this earlier.

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:04.680
I do have a slight imbalance between malignant
and benign.

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:10.410
So what
I'm going to start to do now is leverage Python

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:13.990
code to do a decision tree.

00:15:13.990 --> 00:15:16.990
So we've analyzed our data here.

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:23.450
I'm going to show you that rows 200 through
210 look very similar.

00:15:23.450 --> 00:15:24.450
Right?

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:26.800
And
then I'm going to have Python do some basic

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:29.000
pre-processing for me.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:33.339
So if you haven't used Python for data
science, this is one of the most important

00:15:33.339 --> 00:15:36.019
packages available, scikit-learn.

00:15:36.019 --> 00:15:38.970
So when I run this, I'm going to go
ahead and do the install.

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:40.970
Hopefully, I've already got it installed.

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:41.970
Yes, I do.

00:15:41.970 --> 00:15:42.970
Great.

00:15:42.970 --> 00:15:47.200
And now I'm going to do what's called pre-processing.

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:50.380
So I'm calling that pre-processing function
from scikit-learn,

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:52.690
and I'm doing what's called a labelling code.

00:15:52.690 --> 00:15:56.180
And if you look up here, you can see my diagnosis
is either malignant

00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:57.269
or benign.

00:15:57.269 --> 00:16:01.440
But to a computer, it's easier for systems
to understand if it's a one or a zero.

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:02.440
It's binary.

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:03.440
It's on, off.

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:04.490
So
it makes our processing faster, and it makes

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:06.370
it easier to use certain algorithms.

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:10.870
So the first thing I'm going to do is check
