technologies that I think are really fascinating
and that I'll leave 10 minute at the end for

00:46:53.930 --> 00:46:55.710
questions.

00:46:55.710 --> 00:47:00.590
So one of the
other most important things that I think we

00:47:00.590 --> 00:47:04.220
have to really focus on is genomics.

00:47:04.220 --> 00:47:09.780
I don't know if any of you are or in
the genomics field, but one of my favorite

00:47:09.780 --> 00:47:11.800
packages is something called Adam.

00:47:11.800 --> 00:47:18.030
So if you go out there and you look up Apache
Adam-- A-D-A-M-- you'll see that there is

00:47:18.030 --> 00:47:23.900
actually an open source
distribution that runs in Spark, meaning highly

00:47:23.900 --> 00:47:29.100
saveable, that's able to do certain genomic
staff.

00:47:29.100 --> 00:47:35.200
Now, I am by no
means a geneticist here, but what I'll show

00:47:35.200 --> 00:47:41.060
you here is something called counting k-mers,
K-M-E-R-S, something

00:47:41.060 --> 00:47:47.870
that previously took, I don't know, a year
to do, we can now do divide and conquer, spread

00:47:47.870 --> 00:47:54.910
across multiple ECs or
cloud systems, and produce tangible results.

00:47:54.910 --> 00:48:01.810
So here's-- if I say a 10, where k is 10,
looking for strings of 10 and counting them,

00:48:01.810 --> 00:48:04.580
and this is actually doing it get
out of a mouse's genome.

00:48:04.580 --> 00:48:09.890
So this is something that's obviously not
as pretty as some of the other stuff you've

00:48:09.890 --> 00:48:16.090
seen, but if things we're working on an IBM,
how do we work with the FDA to look at the

00:48:16.090 --> 00:48:20.851
genetics of certain trends
or chemical formulas breaking down the new

00:48:20.851 --> 00:48:22.080
drug safety type stuff.

00:48:22.080 --> 00:48:23.970
So fascinating stuff there.

00:48:23.970 --> 00:48:27.080
Again, that
technology, if I can give them a plug, is

00:48:27.080 --> 00:48:30.540
called Adam, A-D-A-M, and if you're a geneticist
I highly recommend you

00:48:30.540 --> 00:48:31.540
look into it.

00:48:31.540 --> 00:48:32.540
Good stuff there.

00:48:32.540 --> 00:48:39.590
And the last piece that I'll bring up is something
which may seem trivial, but it's actually

00:48:39.590 --> 00:48:41.240
super important.

00:48:41.240 --> 00:48:45.050
We all
work in environments where we have constrained

00:48:45.050 --> 00:48:46.670
resources.

00:48:46.670 --> 00:48:49.550
And so the other side of predictive analytics
is

00:48:49.550 --> 00:48:51.740
something called prescriptive analytics.

00:48:51.740 --> 00:48:56.790
If I can predict that I'm going to need a
certain amount of the drug or

00:48:56.790 --> 00:49:01.510
anything like that, then I need to know what
to do about it next.

00:49:01.510 --> 00:49:05.970
So this is something-- it's a classic computer
science problem called the nurse assignment

00:49:05.970 --> 00:49:06.970
problem.

00:49:06.970 --> 00:49:12.150
And here
we're combining Python with IBM's resource

00:49:12.150 --> 00:49:13.720
optimization engine.

00:49:13.720 --> 00:49:21.080
So I need to schedule all these nurses together,
I load in their data, and they each have specialties.

00:49:21.080 --> 00:49:23.300
They each have vacation times.

00:49:23.300 --> 00:49:25.770
They each have costs
associated with them.

00:49:25.770 --> 00:49:28.890
Some of them are in different locations, some
of them don't like each other and don't work

00:49:28.890 --> 00:49:30.020
with each other.

00:49:30.020 --> 00:49:33.810
That's exactly what a prescriptive analytics
engine does.

00:49:33.810 --> 00:49:39.600
If you give it data about what you're
trying to accomplish, and then you give it

00:49:39.600 --> 00:49:44.300
resource constraints, and we find the most
optimal way to do that

00:49:44.300 --> 00:49:48.730
scheduling, or to have the right drugs at
the right hospital at the right time based

00:49:48.730 --> 00:49:50.450
on these types of things.

00:49:50.450 --> 00:49:56.260
So a really important part of predictive analytics,
something which can be absolutely integrated

00:49:56.260 --> 00:50:02.900
in with the open
source technologies like Python and R, and

00:50:02.900 --> 00:50:06.350
then built out so that you can actually do
these prescriptions or

00:50:06.350 --> 00:50:09.350
prescriptive analytics on the fly.

00:50:09.350 --> 00:50:12.290
So putting the right vehicles in the right
place.

00:50:12.290 --> 00:50:14.780
This is something that I've
personally spent a lot of time with, with

00:50:14.780 --> 00:50:19.940
logistics, groups like the Army, Navy, that
kind of stuff.

00:50:19.940 --> 00:50:25.170
