00:50:41.670 --> 00:50:47.720
We have a question where one of our listeners
was asking about how the code

00:50:47.720 --> 00:50:52.210
defines the feature importance score in your
decision tree.

00:50:52.210 --> 00:50:54.560
Yeah, great question.

00:50:54.560 --> 00:50:58.840
So let me go back to it for you here.

00:50:58.840 --> 00:51:04.110
When you do things like decision trees, you
get feature

00:51:04.110 --> 00:51:08.400
importance, because it does really one of
two things.

00:51:08.400 --> 00:51:11.190
So this is something you can go and look up
later.

00:51:11.190 --> 00:51:14.660
But it
either uses what's called a Gini index or

00:51:14.660 --> 00:51:17.440
an entropy score.

00:51:17.440 --> 00:51:25.270
And what it does is it determines what the
most

00:51:25.270 --> 00:51:29.910
important part of that dataset is, because
it's able to split the data based on it.

00:51:29.910 --> 00:51:37.820
So-- let me just bring up my model here--
part of that library is what we were talking

00:51:37.820 --> 00:51:42.090
about, and I'll get down to the
exact spot.

00:51:42.090 --> 00:51:49.100
But what it's realized here is that this concave
points mean, if it's greater than or equal

00:51:49.100 --> 00:51:53.182
to this, you're
almost always in a certain category.

00:51:53.182 --> 00:51:57.180
Another easy analog might be gender.

00:51:57.180 --> 00:52:00.790
If you're trying of determine if
somebody is a male or a female, you could

00:52:00.790 --> 00:52:05.110
use data like how much do they weigh, how
tall are they, but I think if

00:52:05.110 --> 00:52:12.250
you just looked at their physiology, that
give you hands down the biggest clue.

00:52:12.250 --> 00:52:13.250
Maybe not all the time.

00:52:13.250 --> 00:52:17.990
But it's an example of feature importance
there would be, we're at the root node of

00:52:17.990 --> 00:52:22.980
the tree, and it's figured out
that pretty much anything to the left or right

00:52:22.980 --> 00:52:27.320
is going to have a huge impact on whether
in this case it's benign or

00:52:27.320 --> 00:52:28.320
cancerous.

00:52:28.320 --> 00:52:36.600
So let's see-- the actual code part that gives
you that output is right here.

00:52:36.600 --> 00:52:41.840
So we're doing this part of
the algorithm, we're getting the score, of,

00:52:41.840 --> 00:52:47.550
the feature score, and then sorting it by
how high that score is.

00:52:47.550 --> 00:52:51.900
So 10,
obviously, in a descending.

00:52:51.900 --> 00:52:55.800
Hopefully that answers the question.

